  "claim": {
    "summary": "Schmidhuber argues that ACM's justification and the Turing Lecture credit the three laureates for methods first published by others, among them Ivakhnenko and Lapa's deep networks (1965), Amari's stochastic gradient descent for multilayer networks (1967 to 1968), Linnainmaa's backpropagation (1970), Fukushima's convolutional architecture and rectified linear units (1969 to 1979), and results from his own group on LSTM, attention, GANs and related methods. He says the laureates did not cite these works, even in later surveys, and that they credited each other instead. He published a critique of the ACM statement on 25 June 2020, expanded it on 24 September 2021 (IDSIA-77-21) after the lecture appeared, and collected more than a dozen concrete priority disputes in a report of 14 December 2023 (IDSIA-23-23).",
