  "response": {
    "summary": "I found no point-by-point reply from ACM or from the three laureates, so the statements below address parts of the dispute. Hinton's Reddit reply of April 2020, written about the Honda Prize, says he has not claimed to have invented backpropagation and that he and his co-authors did not know the history in 1986 and failed to cite earlier inventors. LeCun told Analytics India Magazine in December 2022 that Schmidhuber has an unusual idea of how credit should be attributed, namely that whoever has the germ of an idea gets all the credit for what follows, and that making ideas work at scale is a separate contribution. On the Connectionists mailing list in November 2021, Randall O'Reilly argued that plagiarism implies willful stealing and that many of the cases involve reinvention, while Stephen José Hanson, whose 1990 stochastic delta rule is one of the works Schmidhuber cites, wrote that to popularize is not to invent.",
    "quotes": [
      {
        "who": "Geoffrey Hinton (Reddit)",
        "text": "It is true that when we first published we did not know the history so there were previous inventors that we failed to cite",
        "date": "2020-04-23",
        "url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20210824042207/https://www.reddit.com/r/MachineLearning/comments/g5ali0/d_schmidhuber_critique_of_honda_prize_for_dr/"
      },
      {
        "who": "Yann LeCun (interview with Analytics India Magazine)",
        "text": "He has an unusual idea about how credit should be attributed.",
        "date": "2022-12-13",
        "url": "https://analyticsindiamag.com/ai-features/angels-demons-of-ai"
      },
      {
        "who": "Yann LeCun (interview with Analytics India Magazine)",
        "text": "if someone has the germ of an idea, they should get all the credit for what comes after.",
        "date": "2022-12-13",
        "url": "https://analyticsindiamag.com/ai-features/angels-demons-of-ai"
      },
      {
        "who": "Randall O'Reilly (Connectionists mailing list)",
        "text": "Using the term plagiarize here implies a willful stealing of other people's ideas, and is a very serious allegation as I'm sure you are aware.",
        "date": "2021-11-14",
        "url": "https://mailman.srv.cs.cmu.edu/pipermail/connectionists/2021-November/036874.html"
      },
      {
        "who": "Stephen José Hanson (Connectionists mailing list)",
        "text": "to popularize is not to invent. Many of Juergen's concerns could be solved with some scholarship",
        "date": "2021-10-28",
        "url": "https://mailman.srv.cs.cmu.edu/pipermail/connectionists/2021-October/036742.html"
      }
    ]
  },
  "agreed_facts": [
    "ACM announced the 2018 A.M. Turing Award for Bengio, Hinton and LeCun on 27 March 2019, and the three published a Turing Lecture on deep learning in Communications of the ACM in July 2021.",
    "Schmidhuber published his critique of the ACM statement on 25 June 2020, his report on the Turing Lecture (IDSIA-77-21) on 24 September 2021 and his report on more than a dozen priority disputes (IDSIA-23-23) on 14 December 2023.",
    "Linnainmaa's 1970 thesis, Ivakhnenko's work of 1965 to 1971 and Fukushima's neocognitron of 1979 to 1980 were published before the laureates' papers on the same topics.",
    "Hinton wrote in 2020 that earlier inventors of backpropagation existed whom the authors of the 1986 paper did not cite.",
    "ACM's announcement dates the introduction of neural networks as a tool for pattern recognition to the 1980s, a statement that Schmidhuber's reports dispute."
  ],
  "crux": "The sources differ on whether the ACM citation and the Turing Lecture misstate the origins of deep learning by leaving out earlier work, or fairly describe the laureates' own contributions in experiments, engineering and popularization. They also differ on whether a missing citation in a particular paper amounts to failing to credit the earlier author or to independent reinvention.",
